# ---------- STEP 5 · Buffer and boost
S[5]=dict(lbl="What is an interaction?|What does the buffer do?|What does the boost do?|What did the diary studies find?|What does thriving mean in JD-R?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("How do demands and resources work together, and why do some people thrive under high demands?",
 "Step 4 placed the buffer and the boost in the gold square. Here you look inside it. This is the most direct answer to the theme's opening puzzle: two people with the same high demands can end up in very different places.",
 ["What an interaction is","The buffer: resources soften what demands do to strain","The boost: challenging demands make resources more motivating","The diary studies behind both","What \"thriving\" means in JD-R"]),
CARD("An interaction means the effect of one thing depends on the level of another thing.",
 "Rule 3 says demands and resources have a combined effect on well-being. In a graph you recognize an interaction by lines that are <b>not parallel</b>: the slope for one group differs from the slope for the other. If the lines are parallel, there is only a <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"maineff\">main effect</b>: one group is simply higher or lower everywhere. <i>Our example:</i> coffee keeps you awake much more on a night after little sleep than after a good night's sleep: the effect of coffee depends on your sleep.",
 "in JD-R, you cannot say what a demand does without asking which resources are there.",
 "the first interaction: the buffer."),
CARD("Job resources weaken the effect of job demands on strain: the buffer hypothesis.",
 "The <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"buffer\">buffer hypothesis</b> is about the drain lane. Picture two umbrellas in the same storm: the rain (demands) is identical, but one umbrella (resources) is much bigger. Resources may change how a demand is perceived, soften the reaction to it, or limit the damage afterwards. In the graph, exhaustion rises steeply with demands for people with few resources, and only a little for people with many. <i>Our example:</i> on days with a heavy workload, a project manager with a supportive team is hardly more exhausted than on quiet days; her colleague without support is much more exhausted.",
 "the buffer is about <b>strain</b>: demands still cost energy, but resources limit how much.",
 "the mirror image, on the fuel lane."),
CARD("Challenging job demands strengthen the positive effect of job resources on work engagement: the boost hypothesis.",
 "The <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"boost\">boost hypothesis</b> is about the fuel lane. Feedback and support matter most on days with complex work or time pressure. In short, resources become most important for engagement when demands are high. <i>Our example:</i> a tailwind helps a cyclist far more on a hard climb than on flat ground.",
 "the boost is about <b>engagement</b>, not burnout. An exam option that says \"the boost hypothesis says demands boost burnout\" is wrong.",
 "how do researchers see these effects?"),
CARD("In diary studies, people were more engaged on challenging days only when they also had resources, and hindrances hurt most when resources were scarce.",
 "A <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"diary\">diary study</b> asks the same people a few questions every day or week, so you can compare each person's better and worse days. <b>Study 1:</b> Tadić et al. (2015) followed teachers. <i>What they found:</i> on days with hard, complex work, teachers were more inspired and engaged, <i>but only</i> when they also had daily resources such as feedback and support (boost). On days with hindrances such as role conflict, they were less engaged, especially when resources were scarce (buffer). <b>Challenge demands</b> (such as complex work) cost energy but offer growth; <b>hindrance demands</b> (such as role conflict) only block your goals; step 8 comes back to them. <b>Study 2:</b> Breevaart and Bakker (2018) looked at leadership. <i>What they found:</i> challenge demands went with the most engagement when the leader was transformational (inspiring, considerate, stimulating), and hindrances hurt most when the leader showed little of this. <i>In plain words:</i> a good day needs both the challenge and the help.",
 "both interactions have been found within the same people, day by day. Note: in the core theory the buffer is about strain; for hindrances, the article also uses it for engagement (p. 192).",
 "what does this say about thriving?"),
CARD("In JD-R terms, thriving means high work engagement, which is highest when high challenge demands meet abundant job resources (p. 193).",
 "Think back to Anna and Sam. Both had high demands. Anna had few resources and drained; Sam had many and got engaged. A job with almost no demands does not produce thriving; it can simply be understimulating. <i>Our example:</i> a junior lawyer on an exciting case with a mentor at her side.",
 "to help people thrive, you do not remove all challenge; you add resources so the challenge energizes instead of drains.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Resources soften demands (buffer, about strain), and challenging demands make resources count (boost, about engagement).",
 [("What is an interaction?","The effect of one thing depends on another; in a graph, lines that are not parallel."),("What does the buffer do?","Resources weaken the effect of demands on strain."),("What does the boost do?","Challenging demands strengthen the effect of resources on engagement."),("What did the diary studies find?","Teachers (Tadić et al., 2015) and leadership (Breevaart & Bakker, 2018): challenge plus resources = most engagement; hindrance without resources = least."),("What is thriving?","High challenge demands met by high resources.")],
 ["A case: with heavy workload, a supported manager is hardly more exhausted; her unsupported colleague is much more exhausted. Which hypothesis? (buffer)","Which statement about the boost is correct? (challenging demands strengthen the resources → engagement link)","Pick the graph that shows a buffer (lines not parallel: a flatter strain line with high resources)"])],
 guides=G("Tap <b>🛡️ Buffer</b> to see the strain graph, then <b>🚀 Boost</b> to see the engagement graph.","In each graph, compare the two lines: which one is steeper?","Say in one sentence what the resources do in each graph, then check the cards."),
 gguide=G("Anna and Sam work on the same ward; only Sam has support. <b>Set today's shift demands</b> with the slider.","Press <b>📓 Log this day</b>. Log at least three different kinds of days.","Answer the two questions about your own graphs, then go on to <b>read the exam graphs</b>: pick the graph that fits each description."))

# ---------- STEP 6 · How they test it
S[6]=dict(lbl="What kinds of evidence stand behind JD-R?|What do meta-analyses show?|What do diary and longitudinal studies add?|Do resources also lower strain directly?|How can a sample hide the buffer?|What else could explain the direct link?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("What kind of evidence do the authors use, and why can a study miss the buffer effect?",
 "The article runs no study of its own, so the real question is: what evidence stands behind its answers? This step also teaches an exam favourite: how a real effect can stay invisible because of who ends up in a sample.",
 ["The three kinds of studies the authors lean on","Why some studies find that resources lower strain directly","How a restricted sample hides the buffer (Figure 1)","The other explanations the authors give"]),
CARD("Three kinds of studies: meta-analyses, diary studies and longitudinal studies.",
 UL([("1","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"meta\">Meta-analysis</b>: pools the results of many earlier studies into one overall result."),("2","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"diary\">Diary study</b>: the same people answer short questions every day or week."),("3","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"longit\">Longitudinal study</b>: the same people are measured at Time 1 and again months or years later.")])+"Each answers a different question: how strong an effect is across many studies, what happens on a person's worse days, and what changes over time.",
 "when you judge a claim, first ask which kind of study it rests on.",
 "what the big meta-analyses show."),
CARD("They show the core of JD-R holds across many studies and thousands of employees.",
 "<b>Crawford et al. (2010)</b> combined 43 studies with almost 27,000 employees. <b>Lesener et al. (2019)</b> combined 57 longitudinal samples (N = 37,324). Because a meta-analysis averages over many samples, its result is usually more reliable than any single study. <i>Our example:</i> one restaurant review can be a fluke; the average of a thousand reviews is hard to argue with.",
 "when the authors say JD-R has \"considerable empirical support\", these meta-analyses are a large part of what they mean.",
 "what the other two designs add."),
CARD("Diary studies show what happens within one person from day to day; longitudinal studies show what changes over months or years.",
 "A <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"within\">within-person</b> effect compares you on your worse days with you on your better days, such as the teacher and nurse studies of steps 4 and 5. A <b>between-person</b> effect compares you with a colleague. Longitudinal studies are needed to test the loops of rules 6 and 7, because a spiral only shows up over time.",
 "each design can see some effects and not others. Spirals need time; daily buffers need daily data.",
 "one finding seemed to break a JD-R rule."),
CARD("Several meta-analyses found a direct link between more job resources and less burnout, which JD-R does not predict: in JD-R, resources relate to strain only through engagement or by buffering demands.",
 "In JD-R, resources work on the fuel lane: resources → engagement → performance. On the drain lane they act through the buffer, not as a direct cause. Yet several meta-analyses found that more job resources go with less burnout directly. The authors give four reasons why earlier studies may have tested this badly. One of them, the fourth in the article, is in the slider game.",
 "a direct link in the data is not automatically a direct effect in reality. You first need to rule out how the study was done.",
 "the first reason: what the sample could see."),
CARD("If almost everyone in a sample has high demands, the low-demand side of the graph is never observed, and the buffer looks like a simple main effect.",
 "Look at Figure 1 (p. 191). With few resources, exhaustion climbs steeply as demands rise; with many resources it barely rises, so the lines are not parallel: that is the buffer. Now take only the high-demand strip on the right. There the high-resource line is simply lower: \"more resources, less exhaustion\", a <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"maineff\">main effect</b>. The <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"interaction\">interaction</b> is masked. This is called <b>restricted variance</b> (too little spread in demands). The same happens if the interaction term is left out of the analysis. <i>Our example:</i> judging umbrellas only on rainy days: you see that people with umbrellas are drier, but not that on dry days umbrellas make no difference.",
 "no interaction in a study does not mean no buffer. Always ask which part of the graph the sample could see.",
 "the other three reasons."),
CARD("Three more: the path through engagement was not modelled, burnout was measured with motivation items, and people with more resources may face fewer demands.",
 UL([("1","The indirect path <b>resources → engagement</b> was not included, so its effect shows up as a direct link."),("2","Overall burnout measures include motivation markers, cynicism and reduced accomplishment, which belong to the fuel lane."),("3","Employees with more resources may face, or perceive, fewer demands, so the effect really runs through demands.")])+"(Reason 4 is the masking on the previous card.) But the authors also say the direct link may be real: a direct effect \"cannot be ruled out\". Energetic colleagues may pass on energy, and resources may build personal resources linked to good health.",
 "a direct link between resources and strain is not proof that resources work like demands, but the question is still open.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("JD-R rests on meta-analyses, diary and longitudinal studies; and a study can only see what its sample and analysis allow.",
 [("What kinds of evidence?","Meta-analyses, diary studies, longitudinal studies."),("What do meta-analyses show?","Broad support, e.g. Crawford et al. (2010): 43 studies, almost 27,000 employees."),("What do diary and longitudinal studies add?","Within-person day-to-day effects; change over time (needed for spirals)."),("Do resources lower strain directly?","Several meta-analyses find a direct link; JD-R predicts it only indirectly."),("How can a sample hide the buffer?","Only high demands observed (or no interaction term) → the interaction looks like a main effect (Figure 1)."),("Other explanations?","Unmodelled engagement path; motivation items in burnout; more resources, fewer demands.")],
 ["A study of emergency nurses (all high workload) finds support linked to less exhaustion but no interaction. Explain. (restricted variance in demands masks the buffer)","Describe what Figure 1 shows (exhaustion rises with demands, far more steeply with low resources: a demands × resources interaction)","Give two reasons why a direct resources → burnout link is not proof that resources work like demands","Design a study that can detect the buffer (full range of demands, interaction term, engagement path, exhaustion items)"])],
 guides=G("<b>Drag the slider</b> to choose the lowest job demands in your sample.","Watch the graph: the part outside your sample fades out.","At the right end, ask yourself: do the two lines still look non-parallel, or just like one line lower than the other? That is the masking."))

# ---------- STEP 7 · JD-R and COR
S[7]=dict(lbl="What is COR theory?|How is JD-R different from COR?|What did JD-R borrow from COR?|What paradox does COR add?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("How is JD-R related to conservation of resources (COR) theory, and what did JD-R borrow from it?",
 "This is the first of the 13 questions, and one you will meet again and again in this theme; meeting 2 gives it a whole reading. JD-R \"stands on the shoulders of giants\": classic job design theories such as Herzberg's two-factor theory, job characteristics theory and Karasek's demand-control model. Knowing its link with COR shows which parts of the answer are about work specifically and which are about people in general.",
 ["What COR theory claims","How JD-R differs from it","The two ideas JD-R borrowed","A paradox COR adds for people under strain"]),
CARD("A general stress theory (Hobfoll, 1989): people try to obtain, retain, foster and protect the resources they value.",
 "The first FAQ compares JD-R with <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"cor\">conservation of resources (COR) theory</b>. In COR, \"resources\" is meant broadly: objects (a car), conditions (a marriage, a job), skills and beliefs (self-efficacy), and <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"volatile\">volatile resources</b> such as energy, attention and time. COR is basically about what people do when they expect to lose, or actually lose, resources. People must invest resources to protect against loss, recover from loss and gain new ones. <i>Our example:</i> someone who fears losing their job starts working extra evenings, spending time and energy to protect the job.",
 "in COR, stress is about losing, or fearing to lose, what you value, in any part of life.",
 "where JD-R zooms in."),
CARD("JD-R is more explicit: it says which type of resources, namely resources in the work environment (job resources), are needed to deal with job demands and protect well-being.",
 "COR is broad: it covers people and all the resources they value in any life domain. JD-R is an organizational theory about job demands and job resources (which ones depends on the job), and how they affect burnout, engagement and performance. <i>Our example:</i> COR is a general map of your whole life's battery; JD-R is the detailed floor plan of your workplace.",
 "the two are family, not rivals. The difference is scope and focus, not opposite predictions.",
 "what JD-R took from its parent."),
CARD("Two ideas: COR's gain paradox, which underlies the boost, and loss and gain spirals.",
 UL([("1","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"gainpar\">Gain paradox principle</b>: gaining resources becomes more important when you are losing them. It is relevant for the buffer hypothesis (which itself was first based on Karasek's demand-control model) and the direct basis of the boost hypothesis: resources matter most on high-demand days."),("2","<b>Spirals:</b> COR describes loss and gain spirals. JD-R spells them out for work: demands → strain → self-undermining → more demands, and resources → engagement → job crafting → more resources. Research suggests the loss cycle is stronger for people already high on chronic burnout, and the gain cycle for people already high on engagement.")]),
 "if an exam asks what the boost hypothesis comes from, the answer is COR's gain paradox.",
 "one more lesson from COR."),
CARD("On high-demand days you need to actively seek new resources, exactly when you have the least energy to do so.",
 "COR adds a paradox that JD-R must face. When demands pile up, finding support or feedback would help most, but strain makes it harder to go and get them. <i>Our example:</i> in exam week, a student who most needs a study group is the one too tired to organize it.",
 "people under strain may need help to find resources. The article calls this seeking expansion-oriented job crafting (rule 6, the ladder), and it is hardest exactly when energy is low.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("COR is the broad parent theory about all valued resources; JD-R is the work-specific child that borrowed the gain paradox and the spirals.",
 [("What is COR?","Hobfoll (1989): people obtain, retain, foster and protect valued resources; stress comes from loss or threatened loss."),("How does JD-R differ?","More explicit: job resources are what you need to deal with job demands."),("What did JD-R borrow?","The gain paradox (the basis of the boost, relevant to the buffer) and loss and gain spirals, made concrete."),("What paradox does COR add?","You must seek resources exactly when you have least energy.")],
 ["Which COR principle is the direct basis of the boost hypothesis? (the gain paradox)","Which statement is correct? \"JD-R and COR are rival theories with opposite predictions.\" (false: JD-R builds on COR)","Is this a COR idea, a JD-R idea, or both? (for example: spirals = both, made concrete in JD-R)"])],
 gguide=G("Read the statement on the card.","Choose <b>COR theory</b>, <b>JD-R theory</b> or <b>Both</b>.","Read why, then go to the next statement. Seven statements in total."))

# ---------- STEP 8 · Drawing the lines
S[8]=dict(lbl="Can a resource be a demand?|Is low support a demand?|Are all demands the same?|How do demands and resources relate?|How strongly are engagement and burnout related?|What else sets them apart?|Why does this matter in practice?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("Where does one JD-R concept end and the next begin?",
 "A theory with two ingredients and two outcomes only works if you can tell them apart. Three of the FAQs check exactly these boundaries, and exams love them because each one has a tempting wrong answer.",
 ["Whether a resource can also be a demand","Whether a low resource counts as a demand","Whether all demands are alike: challenges and hindrances","Whether work engagement is simply the opposite of burnout"]),
CARD("No: demands cost energy and drive the health impairment process; resources spark motivation and drive the motivational process.",
 "The two ingredients do different jobs. A feature can look like both only when it shows up as two different behaviours. Leadership is the classic tricky case. Transformational leadership (inspiring and supporting people) acts as a resource; abusive supervision (for example, humiliating people in public) acts as a demand. A meta-analysis (Pletzer et al., 2024) found that each has its own unique effects on engagement, burnout and performance. <i>In plain words:</i> they are two different behaviours, not two ends of one scale.",
 "when a feature seems to be both, look closer: you are usually looking at two different behaviours.",
 "what about having very little of a resource?"),
CARD("No: a low score on a resource is not a high score on a demand, and low demands are not a resource.",
 "Little support makes you less engaged, but it does not itself cost effort the way a demand does. And a job with almost no workload is not a resource; it can simply be understimulating. <i>Our example:</i> an empty cup is not a storm. Missing feedback is an empty cup (a lack of a resource); a flood of emails is the storm (a demand).",
 "an exam option that says \"an employee who gets little feedback has a high job demand\" is wrong.",
 "are all demands equally bad?"),
CARD("No: challenge demands offer growth, hindrance demands block your goals; both cost energy, but only hindrances undermine engagement.",
 "The <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"chf\">challenge–hindrance framework</b> (Cavanaugh et al., 2000; Podsakoff et al., 2023) splits the demand pile:"+UL([("↑","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"challenge\">Challenge demands</b>: complexity, time pressure, responsibility."),("↓","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"hindrance\">Hindrance demands</b>: role ambiguity, administrative hassles, interpersonal conflict.")])+"Both can cause strain when too high or chronic; challenges can also add positive effects. The split is subjective. <b>The study:</b> Bakker and Sanz-Vergel (2013) found that nurses saw work pressure more as a hindrance than a challenge, and the reverse for emotional demands. <i>In plain words:</i> the job sector shapes how a demand is felt.",
 "the same demand can be a challenge for one group and a hindrance for another; ask the people who do the job.",
 "how demands and resources usually go together."),
CARD("Usually negatively: people with high demands tend to have fewer resources, though some jobs have high levels of both.",
 "Busy people often have no time to collect feedback or support. <b>The studies:</b> Lesener et al. (2019) found negative links at the same time and over time; Jong and Ford (2016), with almost 300,000 U.S. federal employees in 38 agencies, found that workload lowered later resources and resources lowered later demands. But among highly educated employees, Bakker et al. (2004) found positive links; managers, for example, often have high demands <i>and</i> high resources. <i>Our example:</i> a surgeon with a heavy schedule and an excellent team.",
 "demands and resources are separate dials. Do not assume one tells you the other.",
 "the outcomes: two dials as well?"),
CARD("Only moderately: three meta-analyses found correlations between about −.25 and −.37, far from perfect opposites.",
 "<b>Work engagement</b> is vigor, dedication and absorption; <b>burnout</b> is exhaustion, cynicism and reduced efficacy. A <b>correlation</b> (r) runs from −1 to +1 and shows how strongly two things go together; −1 would mean perfect opposites. The three meta-analyses:"+UL([("r","−.37 (Crawford et al., 2010)"),("r","−.25 (Nahrgang et al., 2011)"),("r","−.36 (Lesener et al., 2019)")]),
 "engagement and burnout share only a limited part of their variation: they are separate states, not two ends of one scale.",
 "what else sets them apart?"),
CARD("Different predictors and different outcomes, and a person can be low on both.",
 "Engagement is predicted by abundant resources plus challenges; burnout by high demands plus a lack of resources. Engagement predicts performance better. Someone can be low on both: not burned out, but not engaged either, which the authors link to indifference, the attitude behind \"quiet quitting\". In the circumplex picture, engagement sits in the active, positive corner and burnout on the passive, negative side.",
 "JD-R needs two separate outcome measures, not one scale from \"burned out\" to \"engaged\".",
 "why the boundaries matter for interventions."),
CARD("Because fixing one side does not fix the other: removing a hindrance can reduce burnout without creating engagement.",
 "<i>Our application:</i> if burnout and engagement were one scale, lowering burnout would automatically raise engagement. They are not, so an organization needs two kinds of action: reduce hindrances and excessive demands to prevent burnout, and add resources and challenges to build engagement. <i>Our example:</i> cutting red tape may stop a team from burning out, but they still need feedback and interesting work to become engaged.",
 "always ask which outcome an intervention targets.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("A low resource is not a demand, challenges differ from hindrances, and engagement is not simply the absence of burnout.",
 [("Can a resource be a demand?","No; leadership splits into two behaviours (transformational = resource, abusive = demand)."),("Is low support a demand?","No; a lack of a resource lowers engagement but is not a demand; low demands are not a resource."),("Are all demands the same?","No; challenges (time pressure) vs hindrances (role ambiguity); the split is subjective (nurses)."),("How do demands and resources relate?","Usually negatively, with exceptions such as managers."),("Is engagement the opposite of burnout?","No; r = −.25 to −.37, different predictors and outcomes; low on both = indifferent."),("Why does it matter?","Reducing burnout does not create engagement by itself.")],
 ["Give two arguments that engagement is not the opposite of burnout (moderate correlations; unique predictors and outcomes)","Classify: time pressure, role ambiguity, administrative hassles (challenge, hindrance, hindrance)","A trap: \"Little feedback is a high job demand.\" (false)"])],
 gguide=G("Read the item on the card. Some are traps.","Choose <b>demand</b>, <b>resource</b> or <b>neither</b>, applying the authors' answers exactly.","Read why, then go to the next item."))

# ---------- STEP 9 · Loss and gain spirals
S[9]=dict(lbl="What is a loss cycle?|What is a gain spiral?|When is a loop a real spiral?|What evidence suggests a loss spiral?|What evidence suggests a gain spiral?|Why is the evidence still limited?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("Do loss and gain spirals really exist, and how strong is the evidence?",
 "So far everything happened at one moment. But work goes on week after week, and rules 6 and 7 say the processes feed back on themselves. This adds time to the theme question: burning out and thriving can snowball. It is also the FAQ where the authors are most self-critical.",
 ["The loss cycle and the gain spiral","The two conditions for a real spiral","The studies that suggest spirals","Why the authors call the evidence limited"]),
CARD("Demands cause strain, strained people self-undermine, and that creates even more demands.",
 "A <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"spiral\">spiral</b> is a self-reinforcing loop: the end of the chain makes its start stronger. In the loss cycle, <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"selfund\">self-undermining</b> (careless mistakes, poor communication, conflicts) is the link that feeds strain back into demands. <i>Our example:</i> a student skips sleep to meet deadlines, makes mistakes, and then has to redo work, which means even less sleep.",
 "in the loss cycle, the person's own behaviour under strain makes the job harder.",
 "the mirror image."),
CARD("Resources spark engagement, engaged people craft their job, and that creates even more resources.",
 "In the gain spiral, <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"crafting\">job crafting</b> is the link that feeds engagement back into resources: asking for feedback, seeking support, starting a new project. <i>Our example:</i> an engaged trainee volunteers for a project, meets a mentor there, and gets more support for the next one.",
 "drained people dig deeper; engaged people build higher.",
 "a loop is not yet a spiral."),
CARD("When the variables influence each other in both directions and their levels keep going down (loss) or up (gain) over time.",
 "These are the two conditions of Salanova et al. (2010):"+UL([("1","<b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"recip\">Reciprocal</b> relations: A affects B and B affects A."),("2","The absolute levels keep falling or rising over time.")])+"A loop that just stays at the same level is not a spiral. That is what the \"One more round\" button shows: in a real spiral, the bars keep moving in one direction.",
 "reciprocity alone is not enough. An exam option that says \"any reciprocal relation proves a spiral\" is wrong.",
 "what the studies show for loss."),
CARD("Three studies the authors cite: strain fed into future demands and later burnout, especially for people already high on burnout.",
 "<b>Study 1:</b> Bakker et al. (2000) followed general practitioners. <i>What they found:</i> doctors who distanced themselves from patients (depersonalization) faced more demanding patients five years later, even after controlling for their earlier patient demands. <b>Study 2:</b> ten Brummelhuis et al. (2011) followed financial consultants. <i>What they found:</i> burnout predicted burnout two years later, through more demands and fewer resources. <b>Study 3:</b> Bakker, Xanthopoulou and Demerouti (2023) followed employees over five working weeks. <i>What they found:</i> weekly demands were related to self-undermining through burnout symptoms, more strongly for people already high on chronic burnout. <i>In plain words:</i> strain today can make tomorrow's job harder.",
 "the authors cite these three studies as suggesting a loss spiral, not as proof.",
 "and for gain?"),
CARD("Reciprocal links between resources, engagement and initiative; but not every positive loop counts.",
 "<b>The study:</b> Hakanen et al. (2008) found reciprocal links between job resources, work engagement and personal initiative. <i>In plain words:</i> each fed the others over time. <b>Not enough:</b> a study showing that positive affect predicts broad-minded coping (looking at a problem from many angles) over time (Fredrickson &amp; Joiner, 2018) is <i>not</i> spiral evidence, because the levels did not necessarily rise. The authors add that spirals may show up in specific groups, such as newcomers, but that still needs testing.",
 "for a gain spiral, check both conditions: both directions, and rising levels.",
 "the authors' verdict."),
CARD("Spirals are \"appealing and intuitive\", say the authors, but there is \"relatively little empirical support\".",
 "Two reasons. Reciprocal effects are challenging to test in terms of research design: for example, floor and ceiling effects (scores that cannot drop or rise further on a scale). And <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"contagion\">contagion</b>, colleagues passing stress or energy to each other, can create the same statistics without a spiral.",
 "when you write about spirals, always add the authors' own verdict: limited evidence so far.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("Loss cycle: demands → strain → self-undermining → more demands. Gain spiral: resources → engagement → crafting → more resources. Appealing, but the evidence is still limited.",
 [("What is a loss cycle?","Strain leads to self-undermining, which creates more demands."),("What is a gain spiral?","Engagement leads to job crafting, which creates more resources."),("When is it a real spiral?","Both directions and levels that keep falling or rising (Salanova et al., 2010)."),("Loss evidence?","GPs over five years; financial consultants over two years; five weekly measurements."),("Gain evidence?","Hakanen et al. (2008); Fredrickson & Joiner (2018) is not enough."),("Why limited?","Hard to test (floor and ceiling effects); contagion can mimic spirals.")],
 ["Describe the loss cycle and name the behaviour that links strain back to demands (self-undermining)","Which finding is not evidence for a gain spiral? (reciprocity without rising levels)","What two conditions must a spiral meet?"])],
 guides=G("Choose <b>🔻 Loss cycle</b> or <b>🔺 Gain spiral</b>.","Press <b>↻ One more round</b> a few times and watch the bars.","Ask: do the levels keep moving in one direction? That is what makes a loop a spiral. Press <b>Reset</b> to try the other loop."),
 gguide=G("Read the finding on the card.","Decide how the authors treat it: <b>cited as suggesting a spiral</b>, or <b>not enough for a spiral</b>.","Read why, then go to the next finding."))

# ---------- STEP 10 · Limits and reach
S[10]=dict(lbl="Can JD-R be proven wrong?|Where does personality come in?|Do the effects differ within and between people?|Is there one standard questionnaire?|Does JD-R hold across cultures?|Does JD-R work outside the office?|What can you now answer?",cards=[
Q("How far does JD-R reach: can it be proven wrong, and does it hold for different people, cultures and settings?",
 "The core of the theory and its biggest questions are behind you. The last FAQs test its limits. These are typical \"evaluate the theory\" exam questions, and the verdict table and the nine predictions below the cards give you the details.",
 ["Whether JD-R can be falsified","Where personality comes in, and within versus between people","How to measure demands and resources","Whether it holds across cultures and outside work"]),
CARD("Yes, the authors argue: JD-R makes specific, less obvious predictions that sound research could disconfirm.",
 "A theory is <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"falsify\">falsifiable</b> when a study could, in principle, prove it wrong: a sign of a good scientific theory. Critics say JD-R's predictions are so obvious that studies always confirm them. The authors reply: \"there is nothing wrong with a theory that is intuitively reasonable\" (Ajzen, 2020, p. 321); JD-R changed when evidence demanded it (reciprocal findings led to the self-undermining and spiral propositions); and they list nine testable predictions, labelled (a) to (i), such as exhaustion mediating the effect of demands on performance, and job crafting mediating the effect of engagement on resources.",
 "obvious is not the same as untestable. The table of nine predictions below gives each one with a nickname to help you remember; in the exam, expect the description, not the nickname.",
 "where individual people come in."),
CARD("As a stable moderator: personality changes how strongly daily demands and resources affect you.",
 "<b>Personality</b> means your stable traits, such as being calm or easily worried. In the <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"multilevel\">multilevel version</b> of JD-R, stable traits can weaken the daily chain demands → exhaustion → self-undermining and strengthen the chain resources → engagement → proactive behaviour. <b>Study 1:</b> Debusscher et al. (2016). <i>What they found:</i> people high in emotional stability reacted less negatively to momentary demands. <b>Study 2:</b> Scharp et al. (2019). <i>What they found:</i> open people got more engagement and creativity out of playful work design (making work tasks more fun or challenging for yourself). Still open: whether personality changes what a feature <i>is</i> for you, for example feedback feeling like a threat.",
 "the same day can hit two people differently, and part of that difference is their personality.",
 "good days versus good people."),
CARD("Mostly no: a meta-analysis found similar effects within and between people, with one exception for challenge demands.",
 "A <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"within\">within-person</b> effect compares your good and bad days; a <b class=\"tm\" data-g=\"between\">between-person</b> effect compares you with a colleague. <b>The study:</b> Downes et al. (2021), a meta-analysis. <i>What they found:</i> effects were similar at both levels, but challenge demands caused more strain when they fluctuated a lot. <i>In plain words:</i> a steady challenge is easier to handle than one that comes and goes.",
 "JD-R works for comparing days and for comparing people, but watch out for challenges that swing.",
 "how to measure all this."),
CARD("Partly: common demands and resources can be measured with existing scales, but every job also has its own, so the authors advise two stages.",
 UL([("1","<b>Stage 1:</b> open interviews with people at different levels (management, supervisors, staff) about what costs and what gives energy."),("2","<b>Stage 2:</b> select or develop questionnaires, following validation guidelines.")])+"Existing scales include the JD-R questionnaire, the Utrecht Work Engagement Scale and the Oldenburg Burnout Inventory. Standardizing alone risks missing what is specific to a job. <i>Our example:</i> a standard survey would ask a pilot about workload, but maybe not about weather.",
 "measurement follows from rule 1: a flexible theory needs a flexible measurement plan.",
 "does it work everywhere in the world?"),
CARD("Largely yes: only 4 of 24 cultural moderation tests (17%) found a difference.",
 "<b>Culture</b> means the country or society people live in. A <b>moderation test</b> checks whether culture changes the size of a JD-R effect. Across 120 studies (132 samples), about 101,000 people and five world regions, the core paths held, and only 4 of the 24 tests found that culture changed an effect, in strength only. <b>The study:</b> Rattrie et al. (2020) tested Hofstede's cultural dimensions. <i>What they found:</i> the demands → burnout link was stronger in masculine cultures, the harm of demands was greater in tight societies, and individualism strengthened resources → engagement. Their explanation: masculine and tight cultures value career success, which makes it harder to switch off. Still open: how culture affects job crafting, self-undermining and spirals, and more research outside Western countries.",
 "for culture questions, give the number: 4 of 24 (17%).",
 "beyond paid work."),
CARD("Yes: the same logic has been applied at home, in sport and in education.",
 "The idea that demands use energy and resources trigger motivation can be applied in any life domain (p. 195). At home: the <b>work–home resources model</b> (ten Brummelhuis &amp; Bakker, 2012) says demands and resources in one domain affect energy and optimism, which carry over into the other. JD-R was also recently extended to crises (Demerouti &amp; Bakker, 2023): buffer and boost can cross life domains. In sport: <b>The study:</b> Sorkkila et al. (2020) studied student athletes. <i>What they found:</i> those with more burnout reported more demands (school stress, poor recovery, disempowering coaching); those with less burnout reported more resources, such as support and empowering coaching. Other researchers (not this article) have built a study demands–resources version for students, with teachers as a key resource.",
 "JD-R is a way of thinking about energy and motivation, not only about offices.",
 "check what you can now answer."),
RECAP("JD-R is testable, makes room for personality, mixes standard and job-specific measurement, and largely holds across cultures and settings; its newest parts are its weakest.",
 [("Can it be proven wrong?","Yes: nine testable mediation and moderation predictions; it has been revised on evidence."),("Personality?","A stable moderator of daily processes (emotional stability, openness)."),("Within vs between?","Similar effects; fluctuating challenges cause more strain (Downes et al., 2021)."),("One questionnaire?","Partly: interviews first, then questionnaires."),("Culture?","Only 4 of 24 tests (17%) significant."),("Outside work?","Home, sport, education.")],
 ["Why can JD-R be falsified, according to the authors? (intuitive is fine; nine less obvious predictions; revised on evidence)","What did the cultural moderation tests show? (4 of 24 significant: largely universal)","How should an organization measure its demands and resources? (two stages)","Which prediction is (b) and which is (c)? (buffer is about strain; boost is about engagement)"])],
 gguide=G("Read the imagined new study on the card.","Decide what it would mean: <b>counts against JD-R</b> or <b>consistent with JD-R</b>.","Read why, then go to the next study."))

def run(path):
    s=open(path,encoding='utf-8').read()
    for n,d in S.items():
        s=apply_step(s,W,n,d['lbl'],d['cards'],guides=d.get('guides'),gguide=d.get('gguide'))
    s=s.replace('(all nine are in the table above)','(all nine are in the table further down)')
    open(path,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s);print('ok',list(S))
if __name__=='__main__': run(sys.argv[1])
